AegisMark 2.0 — Review of the revised engineering specification
I've read the entire revised document (524 lines).

My initial reaction is positive: this is a substantial improvement over AegisMark 1.0. Whoever revised it has addressed most of the major architectural problems we identified.

However, I wouldn't approve it for full development just yet.

There are still some important technical mistakes, security gaps, and inconsistencies. A few are particularly serious because they concern the encryption and synchronization mechanisms that are supposed to be AegisMark's strongest selling points.

I'll be a little stricter in this review because we're moving from a product idea toward an actual engineering specification.
