The optional permission point is particularly worth fixing: a permission requested at runtime must be declared as an optional permission in the relevant manifest. It cannot simply be requested dynamically without being declared.

5. Important missing features that I would add
I don't want to turn AegisMark into an unnecessarily complicated application. But there are some features that I consider essential for a trustworthy bookmark manager.

Here are my additions.
5.1 Bookmark integrity and data protection
The document needs a stronger data-integrity strategy.

Add:
- Transactional database operations.
- Automatic database schema versioning.
- Safe migration between application versions.
- Backup validation before restoration.
- Recovery from interrupted imports.
- Duplicate record detection.
- Corrupted-record detection.
- Graceful handling of insufficient storage.
- Protection against accidental bulk deletion.
One feature I particularly recommend is a Trash / Recently Deleted system.

When users delete bookmarks, don't immediately permanently erase them.

Keep them in a recoverable state for a configurable period, and synchronize deletion properly across devices.
5.2 Secure import and export
This deserves more attention because importing bookmark files can introduce malicious data.

The application should:
- Validate imported JSON structures.
- Validate bookmark URLs.
- Reject unsupported or dangerous URL schemes.
- Limit import file sizes.
- Prevent deeply nested or excessively large structures from exhausting memory.
- Avoid executing imported HTML or JavaScript.
- Handle malformed Netscape HTML files safely.
- Detect duplicates.
- Provide an import preview before committing changes.
Also, encrypted JSON and standard Netscape HTML exports are different security products.

The latter is generally plaintext.

The application should warn users before creating an unencrypted export.
5.3 Better privacy controls
I'd add a dedicated Privacy Center with:
Setting	Function
Local-only mode	Disable cloud synchronization
Cloud sync toggle	Enable/disable synchronization
Device list	View authorized devices
Revoke device	Remove device authorization
Export vault	Download user data
Delete cloud data	Request deletion of server-side data
Delete account	Remove account and associated service data
Telemetry	Explicitly disclose any optional diagnostics
Privacy report	Explain exactly what data the server can see

I would make privacy controls understandable to ordinary users rather than displaying cryptographic jargon everywhere.
5.4 Accessibility and usability
The revised specification talks about visual design, but accessibility is still underdeveloped.

Add:
- Full keyboard navigation.
- Proper screen-reader labels.
- WCAG 2.2 AA as a design and testing target.
- Reduced-motion support.
- High-contrast support.
- Responsive layouts.
- Clear error messages.
- Accessible focus indicators.
- Consistent terminology.
- Localization-ready architecture.
One small but important UX detail: don't display cryptographic technical information to ordinary users unless it helps them make a decision.

For example, show:

"Your vault is encrypted and synchronized."

Provide an advanced security view for people who want to inspect encryption details.

6. The biggest architectural issue: the local vault and web dashboard
This is something I think the developer should clarify before implementation.

The specification uses:
- IndexedDB for the local vault.
- Browser extension storage.
- A web dashboard.
- A Safari web companion.
But these are not automatically the same storage environment.

For example:
- Chrome extension IndexedDB belongs to the extension's origin.
- A normal website has a separate IndexedDB database.
- Safari's extension has its own execution and storage environment.
- A PWA has its own origin-specific storage.
Therefore, you cannot simply assume the same vault database is accessible everywhere.

I recommend defining two storage layers:
Layer 1 — Local vault
Encrypted local database

Independent per installation and platform.
- Chrome extension
- Firefox extension
- Safari extension
- Web companion / PWA

Authenticated encrypted synchronization

Layer 2 — Shared cloud vault
Encrypted synchronization relay

Shared account-level synchronization service.
- Encrypted records
- Device authorization
- Synchronization revisions
- Encrypted backup history


This means each installation has an independent local encrypted vault, but authorized devices synchronize through the same encrypted protocol.

This is much more realistic than pretending all browsers can access one shared local database.

7. Testing is still insufficient
Needs substantial improvement
The last section of the revised document is one of my biggest remaining concerns.

It mentions:
- Theme verification.
- End-to-end functional verification.
- Compilation.
- Linting.
These are not sufficient for an application that advertises security as its primary differentiator.

I would introduce a dedicated verification plan.
Testing category	What to test
Unit testing	Encryption, decryption, key derivation, validation
Cryptographic testing	Known-answer tests, IV uniqueness and authentication failures
Property testing	Randomized inputs and data invariants
Integration testing	IndexedDB, backend and synchronization
Conflict testing	Concurrent edits, deletions and offline changes
Recovery testing	Lost passphrase, recovery key and device replacement
Security testing	XSS, injection, authorization and API vulnerabilities
Dependency testing	Vulnerable and malicious packages
Import testing	Malformed, oversized and malicious files
Compatibility testing	Actual supported browsers and devices
Performance testing	Large vaults, low-end phones and synchronization
Accessibility testing	Keyboard, screen readers and contrast
Migration testing	Upgrades from every supported database version

I would also add these acceptance criteria:
1. No bookmark disappears silently during synchronization.
2. A corrupted ciphertext is rejected rather than silently accepted.
3. An unauthorized device cannot access the vault.
4. Revoked devices cannot obtain new synchronization data.
5. Restoring a backup produces a verified vault.
6. Offline edits synchronize correctly after reconnection.
7. Failed database migrations do not destroy the previous usable database.
8. The extension does not transmit plaintext bookmark information to the synchronization backend.
9. A compromised synchronization server cannot silently replace a device's latest known vault state without detection.
10. All supported browser builds pass their respective integration tests.
I would also require an independent security review before the public Pro synchronization service launches.

8. Three additional changes I strongly recommend
There are three decisions I would make before proceeding.

A. Remove Extension Builder from the actual product

The revised document still includes an Extension Builder & Exporter inside the full workspace (lines 426–436).

I don't think ordinary users need this.

Keep packaging and ZIP generation inside your developer build pipeline. The product should focus on bookmarks, vault management and synchronization.

This will reduce unnecessary complexity and make the product feel more professional.

B. Define Firefox's actual Manifest V3 implementation

The specification says Firefox uses an MV2/MV3 adapter, but it doesn't explain the background implementation.

This matters because Chrome uses extension service workers, while Firefox's Manifest V3 implementation uses background scripts/event pages rather than Chrome-style service workers. 

MDN+1

The developer should implement and test separate browser-specific background configurations.

C. Complete the ownership and commercial requirements

The document still doesn't adequately define:
- Written IP assignment.
- Third-party dependency licensing.
- Source repository ownership.
- Production infrastructure ownership.
- Payment provider integration.
- Subscription expiration and cancellation.
- Account deletion and data retention.
- Backup retention and deletion policies.
Also, your original request mentioned a free trial. The revised specification now has Free and Pro plans, but doesn't explicitly define a time-limited trial.

I'd keep the free plan regardless, and decide separately whether a 7- or 14-day Pro trial is commercially worthwhile.

