10. Final verdict
My recommendation
Continue development planning

Approve the direction, not yet the implementation.
AegisMark 2.0 is now a credible architectural foundation. The remaining issues are concentrated in cryptographic correctness, synchronization trust, platform implementation and verification.
Here is how I would proceed:
Decision	My recommendation
Keep AegisMark 2.0 architecture	Yes
Start UI/UX design	Yes
Start local-only MVP development	Yes
Start production cloud synchronization	Not yet
Publish as a secure product	Not yet
Request final 2.1 specification	Yes

One important distinction: you don't need to wait for the entire security architecture to be perfected before building the basic bookmark manager. You can develop the local-only MVP while finalizing the synchronization and recovery protocol. Just make sure the local data model and cryptographic interfaces are designed to accommodate those decisions.

My biggest concern is not the UI, pricing or even the choice of programming language. It is whether the developer can correctly implement the encryption and synchronization protocol rather than merely describe it.

If those two components are engineered properly, AegisMark has the foundation to become a genuinely useful, privacy-focused product.

The goal should be a bookmark manager that feels incredibly simple to its users, even though the engineering underneath it is sophisticated.